WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.148
Before moving day, a careful family does a practice pack.

2
00:00:06.148 --> 00:00:12.136
One empty room, a few boxes, the helper with his clipboard, and a good look at the cards he writes.

3
00:00:12.136 --> 00:00:16.672
If he labels the lamp wrong, they want to know today, not at the new house.

4
00:00:16.917 --> 00:00:19.747
A processor test is that practice pack.

5
00:00:19.747 --> 00:00:29.398
A library called kctfork compiles a few lines of Kotlin in memory, runs KSP and your processor on them, and hands back what happened.

6
00:00:29.398 --> 00:00:32.809
No Gradle project, and a fresh room every time.

7
00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.918
Here's a question many of us never ask out loud.

8
00:00:36.918 --> 00:00:41.802
My test compiles the code, and finds the class the processor should write.

9
00:00:41.802 --> 00:00:42.527
It's green.

10
00:00:42.527 --> 00:00:44.507
So the processor works, right?

11
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.577
Let's find out with the smallest example there is.

12
00:00:48.577 --> 00:00:51.553
A lamp has a name, a number of bulbs, and a shade.

13
00:00:51.553 --> 00:00:56.137
The shade is a String with a question mark, because some lamps have no shade.

14
00:00:56.383 --> 00:01:04.734
The @Builder processor writes LampBuilder: a small class that collects a lamp's values one call at a time, then builds the lamp.

15
00:01:04.734 --> 00:01:08.322
Line 18 sets the shade, and it keeps the question mark.

16
00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:10.014
Now let's plant a bug.

17
00:01:10.014 --> 00:01:15.539
The processor turns each type into KotlinPoet's TypeName through one small function.

18
00:01:15.539 --> 00:01:21.920
The demo hands it a broken one, copy with nullable equals false, which drops every question mark.

19
00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:24.190
So line 18 loses one character.

20
00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:31.631
The smallest mark on the line, doing the heaviest lifting: it's the only thing that says a lamp may have no shade.

21
00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:41.289
Every processor test makes some of four checks: the exit code, the generated text, the messages, and a call into the generated code.

22
00:01:41.289 --> 00:01:46.906
Messages matter when a processor refuses bad input, and this builder refuses nothing.

23
00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:51.268
So the demo reads the text two ways, a quick one and a strict one.

24
00:01:51.268 --> 00:01:53.052
Four checks, weakest first.

25
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:56.098
One, the exit code: how the compile ended.

26
00:01:56.098 --> 00:02:00.762
OK, a compilation error, or an internal error when a processor throws.

27
00:02:00.762 --> 00:02:03.560
Call it the glance: did the helper finish?

28
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:07.234
Two, does the generated text contain class LampBuilder?

29
00:02:07.234 --> 00:02:10.107
The skim: is there a card with the right name?

30
00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:17.767
Three, a golden file: a stored copy of the output you expect, compared with the real output on every run.

31
00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:18.897
The proofreader.

32
00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:20.590
Four, call the code.

33
00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:31.880
kctfork's class loader, the JVM object that loads compiled classes, can see LampBuilder, even though the test was compiled long before LampBuilder existed.

34
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:35.886
So the test can call it, with a lamp that has no shade.

35
00:02:35.886 --> 00:02:36.760
The try out.

36
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.195
Same broken builder, four checks.

37
00:02:39.195 --> 00:02:42.121
Which ones notice the missing question mark?

38
00:02:42.121 --> 00:02:43.185
Pause and guess.

39
00:02:46.583 --> 00:02:49.436
The glance: passes, the exit code is OK.

40
00:02:49.436 --> 00:02:53.573
The skim: passes, the text does contain class LampBuilder.

41
00:02:53.817 --> 00:02:56.453
The proofreader: fails, at line 18.

42
00:02:56.453 --> 00:02:59.390
Expected: shade, String, question mark.

43
00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:01.047
Actual: shade, String.

44
00:03:01.283 --> 00:03:02.974
And the try out: fails.

45
00:03:02.974 --> 00:03:09.958
Calling shade with null throws a NullPointerException: parameter specified as non null is null.

46
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.568
Notice the first two passed honestly.

47
00:03:12.568 --> 00:03:17.815
The broken builder is valid Kotlin, so it compiles, and it does contain the class.

48
00:03:17.815 --> 00:03:22.295
A test made of those two proves one thing: the processor didn't crash.

49
00:03:22.533 --> 00:03:28.679
It's like checking moving day by confirming that a van showed up, with the right name painted on the side.

50
00:03:28.679 --> 00:03:31.405
True, and it tells you nothing about your lamp.

51
00:03:31.405 --> 00:03:35.289
The bug would reach the first family with a lamp that has no shade.

52
00:03:36.233 --> 00:03:37.513
So, the golden file.

53
00:03:37.513 --> 00:03:38.728
It has good habits.

54
00:03:38.728 --> 00:03:46.659
When the file is missing, the check writes it and fails once, so a person reads the new text before it becomes the standard.

55
00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:49.154
It reports the first line that differs.

56
00:03:49.154 --> 00:03:52.736
And UPDATE_GOLDEN equals true rewrites them, on purpose.

57
00:03:52.983 --> 00:03:56.488
But it's a proofreader with no sense of proportion.

58
00:03:56.488 --> 00:04:00.199
A moved space fails as loudly as a lost question mark.

59
00:04:00.199 --> 00:04:10.300
An upgrade that changes the formatting fails every golden file you have, as KotlinPoet 2.4.0 did when it changed how annotation arrays are written.

60
00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:11.399
No bug in sight.

61
00:04:11.633 --> 00:04:13.657
That's why the try out matters.

62
00:04:13.657 --> 00:04:15.941
The golden file catches any change.

63
00:04:15.941 --> 00:04:18.943
The call tells you whether the change matters.

64
00:04:18.943 --> 00:04:19.596
Keep both.

65
00:04:20.533 --> 00:04:21.769
So here's the rule.

66
00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:25.671
A test that only compiles proves the processor didn't crash.

67
00:04:25.671 --> 00:04:27.167
Call the code it wrote.

68
00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:38.520
Here are the four checks in one Kotlin test: the exit code, the text against a golden file, no messages, and a call into the builder, with null for the shade.

69
00:04:38.767 --> 00:04:44.241
Java's annotation processors have the same kind of library, Google's compile-testing.

70
00:04:44.241 --> 00:04:47.140
It checks the compile and the generated file.

71
00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:52.035
To call the code, you test from a module whose own build runs the processor.

72
00:04:52.983 --> 00:04:55.784
So, does a green test mean a working processor?

73
00:04:55.784 --> 00:04:57.333
Only if it calls the code.

74
00:04:57.333 --> 00:05:00.193
The glance and the skim say the helper finished.

75
00:05:00.193 --> 00:05:02.457
The proofreader says the card changed.

76
00:05:02.457 --> 00:05:05.198
The try out says whether the lamp still works.

77
00:05:05.433 --> 00:05:09.509
One more thing about the practice room: it's fresh every time.

78
00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:15.950
A second, incremental build never happens there, so lesson 7's wrong Dependencies pass every test.

79
00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:17.528
Those need a real build.

80
00:05:17.767 --> 00:05:18.553
Quick check.

81
00:05:18.553 --> 00:05:22.943
Fix the bug with copy and no arguments, so the question marks stay.

82
00:05:22.943 --> 00:05:25.040
What do the four checks say now?

83
00:05:28.233 --> 00:05:35.177
The exit code is OK, the text has the class, the golden file says Matches, and shade with null works.

84
00:05:35.177 --> 00:05:36.140
All four pass.
